<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
             xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
             xmlns:vm="clr-namespace:KinesisEdit.ViewModels"
             xmlns:views="clr-namespace:KinesisEdit.Views"
             xmlns:controls="clr-namespace:KinesisEdit.Controls"
             mc:Ignorable="d" d:DesignWidth="300" d:DesignHeight="596"
             x:Class="KinesisEdit.Views.LightingModeRailView"
             x:DataType="vm:LightingTabViewModel">
  <!--
    The Lighting tab's mode rail (mockup 2f), beside the board rather than over it.

    IT IS THE KEY INSPECTOR RAIL'S FRAME, DELIBERATELY. Same `inspectorRail` surface and hairline,
    same `Auto,*,Auto` header/body/footer shape, same `inspectorSection` / `inspectorFooter` blocks
    separated by hairlines rather than by gaps. The two rails are the same object in two tabs, and a
    second rail vocabulary would say they were not.

    IT IS 300 WIDE, NOT 268. `WidthInspectorRailWide` is set here, on the Border, because a local
    value outranks the `inspectorRail` style's own `Width` — the class is paint, not a bridge, so
    widening it at the call site costs the theme nothing. The extra 32 px is what lets the parameter
    footer hold a swatch with its hex, nine speed bars and four direction arrows without wrapping,
    and what lets the colour picker's ring open inside the column instead of over the board.

    THREE THINGS SHARE THE BODY ROW, and only ever two of them are on screen:

      the mode list      fourteen rows, scrolled — the rail is ~600 px tall at the 1000x680 floor
                         and the list alone is taller than that, exactly as the key inspector's
                         panels are.
      the colour picker  DISCLOSED FROM THE SWATCH, in place of the mode list — the precedent is the
                         Tap & hold panel opening §11.6's catalog in place of its two fields. Not a
                         modal, not a flyout, not a third column: the rail nests nothing.
      the parameters     pinned in the footer, never scrolled away. They are the controls this
                         screen exists to drive, and a mode picked at the top of a 600 px list must
                         not push its own speed off the bottom.

    MONO IS THE FILE'S VOICE HERE TOO. `#57C4D8` and `Speed 6 / 9` are values the led file carries;
    the mode names, the parameter summaries and the three section labels are the app talking about
    them. (The section labels are `sectionLabel`, which is mono by role — the design's own 10/600
    caption over a group of controls — and are authored uppercase because Avalonia has no
    text-transform.)
  -->
  <Border Classes="inspectorRail" Width="{DynamicResource WidthInspectorRailWide}">
    <Grid RowDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto">

      <!-- ===== The rail's own header =========================================================
           2f's heading, verbatim, and it is an instruction as much as a title: clicking a row does
           not open a dialog about the mode, it puts the mode on the board. -->
      <Border Grid.Row="0" Classes="inspectorSection">
        <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:LightingTabViewModel.ModeRailCaption}"
                   Classes="cardTitle"
                   TextWrapping="Wrap" />
      </Border>

      <!-- ===== The modes =====================================================================
           One `ModeOption` row per mode the device's firmware offers: the mark, the name, and the
           parameter summary underneath. Both lines are the row's content rather than a template
           part — a Button has one content slot — and `modeSummary` is the class Styles/Editor.axaml
           gives the second line its muted role (and its flip to AccentText on the chosen row).

           Nothing here knows a mode name or a parameter: the membership is
           LightingAvailability's, the caption LightingModeCaptions', and the summary Core's
           LightingModeParameters. -->
      <ScrollViewer Grid.Row="1"
                    HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled"
                    IsVisible="{Binding !$parent[views:LightingModeRailView].IsPickerOpen}">
        <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding Modes}" Margin="8">
          <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
            <ItemsPanelTemplate>
              <StackPanel Spacing="2" />
            </ItemsPanelTemplate>
          </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
          <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
            <DataTemplate DataType="vm:LightingModeViewModel">
              <Button Classes="modeOption"
                      Classes.selected="{Binding IsSelected}"
                      Command="{Binding $parent[views:LightingModeRailView].((vm:LightingTabViewModel)DataContext).SelectModeCommand}"
                      CommandParameter="{Binding}">
                <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*" ColumnSpacing="8">
                  <!-- The mode's mark, which "draws the motion rather than naming it twice"
                       (docs/design/handoff.md). Two Icons in one cell because thirteen of the
                       sixteen marks are outlines and three are solids, and those are different
                       properties on an Icon: the converter answers only the half the mode is drawn
                       in, so the other draws nothing. Stroking a ring *and* filling it would turn
                       it into a disc, which is why they are never both answered. -->
                  <Panel Grid.Column="0"
                         Width="{DynamicResource IconSize}"
                         Height="{DynamicResource IconSize}"
                         VerticalAlignment="Center">
                    <controls:Icon Classes="modeMark"
                                   Classes.selected="{Binding IsSelected}"
                                   Data="{Binding Mode, Converter={StaticResource LightingModeMark}, ConverterParameter=Stroke}" />
                    <controls:Icon Classes="modeMark filled"
                                   Classes.selected="{Binding IsSelected}"
                                   Data="{Binding Mode, Converter={StaticResource LightingModeMark}, ConverterParameter=Fill}" />
                  </Panel>

                  <!-- A Grid column rather than a StackPanel child: a StackPanel measures with
                       infinite width, so a long summary would run off the rail instead of
                       ellipsing inside it. -->
                  <StackPanel Grid.Column="1" Spacing="1" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding Caption}" TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" />
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding Summary}"
                               Classes="modeSummary"
                               TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" />
                  </StackPanel>
                </Grid>
              </Button>
            </DataTemplate>
          </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
        </ItemsControl>
      </ScrollViewer>

      <!-- ===== The colour picker, INSIDE the rail ============================================
           Disclosed by a swatch in the footer below and shown in the body's own row, in place of
           the mode list — the shape the Tap & hold panel already uses for §11.6's catalog. The
           board keeps animating behind it, because nothing here is modal.

           It is the very same ColorPickerViewModel the tab holds, so the ten premixed swatches and
           the twelve `cust_color_N` slots are the ones the Settings tab's strip shares. -->
      <Grid Grid.Row="1"
            RowDefinitions="Auto,*"
            Margin="{DynamicResource PaddingInspectorSection}"
            IsVisible="{Binding $parent[views:LightingModeRailView].IsPickerOpen}">
        <!-- The way out is PINNED, not the last thing in the scroll: the ring, twenty-two swatches
             and the store button are taller than the rail, so a `Done` under them would be below
             the fold every time the picker opened. -->
        <Grid Grid.Row="0" ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" ColumnSpacing="8" Margin="0,0,0,8">
          <TextBlock Grid.Column="0"
                     Text="{x:Static views:LightingModeRailView.PickerLabel}"
                     Classes="sectionLabel"
                     VerticalAlignment="Center" />
          <Button Grid.Column="1"
                  Classes="secondary"
                  Content="{x:Static views:LightingModeRailView.ClosePickerCaption}"
                  Click="OnClosePickerClicked" />
        </Grid>

        <ScrollViewer Grid.Row="1" HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled">
          <controls:ColorPickerView DataContext="{Binding Picker}" />
        </ScrollViewer>
      </Grid>

      <!-- ===== The parameters ================================================================
           2f's "Color `57C4D8`", "Speed 6 / 9" and "Direction ← → ↑ ↓", pinned under the list.
           Each block asks Core what the mode accepts (LightingModeParameters) — except the
           direction row, which is always drawn; see below. -->
      <Border Grid.Row="2" Classes="inspectorFooter">
        <StackPanel Spacing="10">

          <!-- The effect and base swatches. Clicking one points the picker at it (the view model's
               command) AND opens the picker in the body above (the view's own disclosure) — one
               gesture, because a swatch that only re-targeted an invisible picker would look
               dead. -->
          <StackPanel Spacing="6" IsVisible="{Binding Parameters.AcceptsAnyColor}">
            <TextBlock Text="{x:Static views:LightingModeRailView.ColorLabel}" Classes="sectionLabel" />

            <StackPanel Spacing="4">
              <Button Classes="colorSlot"
                      Classes.selected="{Binding EffectColor.IsSelected}"
                      IsVisible="{Binding EffectColor.IsVisible}"
                      HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"
                      HorizontalContentAlignment="Stretch"
                      Command="{Binding SelectColorSlotCommand}"
                      CommandParameter="{Binding EffectColor}"
                      Click="OnColorSlotClicked">
                <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto" ColumnSpacing="8">
                  <!-- The one place a colour legitimately arrives as data rather than as a token:
                       it is the LED colour the file carries, converted by HexColorToBrush. -->
                  <Border Grid.Column="0"
                          Classes="colorSwatchFill"
                          Background="{Binding EffectColor.ColorHex, Converter={StaticResource HexColorToBrush}}" />
                  <TextBlock Grid.Column="1"
                             Text="{Binding EffectColor.Caption}"
                             TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                             VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                  <TextBlock Grid.Column="2"
                             Text="{Binding EffectColor.ColorHex}"
                             Classes="monoValue"
                             VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                </Grid>
              </Button>

              <Button Classes="colorSlot"
                      Classes.selected="{Binding BaseColor.IsSelected}"
                      IsVisible="{Binding BaseColor.IsVisible}"
                      HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"
                      HorizontalContentAlignment="Stretch"
                      Command="{Binding SelectColorSlotCommand}"
                      CommandParameter="{Binding BaseColor}"
                      Click="OnColorSlotClicked">
                <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto" ColumnSpacing="8">
                  <Border Grid.Column="0"
                          Classes="colorSwatchFill"
                          Background="{Binding BaseColor.ColorHex, Converter={StaticResource HexColorToBrush}}" />
                  <TextBlock Grid.Column="1"
                             Text="{Binding BaseColor.Caption}"
                             TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                             VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                  <TextBlock Grid.Column="2"
                             Text="{Binding BaseColor.ColorHex}"
                             Classes="monoValue"
                             VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                </Grid>
              </Button>
            </StackPanel>
          </StackPanel>

          <!-- Speed as nine bars, not a slider: the file's speed is nine discrete values (§2.1) and
               a slider would suggest a continuum. The readout is mono because `6` and `9` are what
               `[spd6]` carries. -->
          <StackPanel Spacing="6" IsVisible="{Binding Parameters.AcceptsSpeed}">
            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*">
              <TextBlock Grid.Column="0"
                         Text="{x:Static views:LightingModeRailView.SpeedLabel}"
                         Classes="sectionLabel"
                         VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              <TextBlock Grid.Column="1"
                         Text="{Binding SpeedControl.Readout}"
                         Classes="monoValue"
                         HorizontalAlignment="Right"
                         VerticalAlignment="Center" />
            </Grid>

            <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding SpeedControl.Segments}">
              <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                  <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="4" />
                </ItemsPanelTemplate>
              </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
              <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                <DataTemplate DataType="vm:LightingSpeedSegmentViewModel">
                  <!-- No content at all: `SpeedBar` is a fixed 10x18 shape with no padding, and the
                       level is read off how many of them are filled. -->
                  <Button Classes="speedBar"
                          Classes.filled="{Binding IsFilled}"
                          Command="{Binding $parent[views:LightingModeRailView].((vm:LightingTabViewModel)DataContext).SetSpeedCommand}"
                          CommandParameter="{Binding Speed}" />
                </DataTemplate>
              </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
            </ItemsControl>
          </StackPanel>

          <!-- ALL FOUR ARROWS, ALWAYS, AND THE ROW ITSELF IS NEVER HIDDEN. This is 2f overriding
               the app's own "absent features are not shown, not disabled" law, in as many words:
               "Directions a mode can't use stay in place, struck through — the row never changes
               shape as you move down the list." The rail is a column the user scrubs, and a
               parameter row that shrank and grew under the pointer would be worse than a struck
               arrow. `.unavailable` is the DirectionSegment theme's own state — the strike, and
               `Focusable`/`IsHitTestVisible` off — not a `:disabled`, because "this mode does not
               run that way" is a fact about the mode rather than the app's willingness.

               The arrow is geometry, never one of `← → ↑ ↓`: neither embedded IBM Plex family is
               guaranteed to carry them, and the icon law is "geometry only". The name behind it
               ("Left", or Rebound's "Horizontal") is the tooltip, so relabelling a mode's axes is
               not lost to a picture. -->
          <StackPanel Spacing="6">
            <TextBlock Text="{x:Static views:LightingModeRailView.DirectionLabel}" Classes="sectionLabel" />

            <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding Directions}">
              <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                  <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="6" />
                </ItemsPanelTemplate>
              </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
              <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                <DataTemplate DataType="vm:LightingDirectionViewModel">
                  <!-- The Icon carries no class: Styles/Editor.axaml colours it from the BUTTON's
                       own classes, so the view writes the state once. -->
                  <Button Classes="directionSegment"
                          Classes.selected="{Binding IsSelected}"
                          Classes.unavailable="{Binding !IsAvailable}"
                          ToolTip.Tip="{Binding Caption}"
                          Command="{Binding $parent[views:LightingModeRailView].((vm:LightingTabViewModel)DataContext).SelectDirectionCommand}"
                          CommandParameter="{Binding}">
                    <controls:Icon Data="{Binding Direction, Converter={StaticResource LightingDirectionMark}}" />
                  </Button>
                </DataTemplate>
              </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
            </ItemsControl>
          </StackPanel>
        </StackPanel>
      </Border>
    </Grid>
  </Border>
</UserControl>
